WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.000
A newborn ferret's eyes have not opened yet.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.780
Even so, its retina is already firing.

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.440
Ganglion cells, the retina's output neurons, fire bursts lasting a few seconds.

4
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.540
Each burst sweeps across the tissue like a wave.

5
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.780
Then a minute or two of silence follows.

6
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.580
Nothing is being seen.

7
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.640
So why send this pattern to the brain?

8
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.240
The wave moves at about 100 micrometres per second.

9
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.580
Two features of it matter for wiring.

10
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.900
Cells near each other fire close together in time.

11
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.840
Cells far apart do not.

12
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.180
So when a cell fires reflects where it sits in the retina.

13
00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:42.263
Now follow the signal to the brain.

14
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.883
Axons from the retina reach the thalamus.

15
00:00:45.543 --> 00:00:49.023
Early in development, axons from the two eyes overlap there.

16
00:00:49.643 --> 00:00:53.863
One thalamic cell hears from neighbouring cells of one eye, and from the other eye.

17
00:00:54.503 --> 00:00:56.743
The two eyes' waves are largely independent.

18
00:00:57.243 --> 00:01:00.143
So the other eye's input fires on its own schedule.

19
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:06.613
In 1949, Donald Hebb proposed a rule for how connections change.

20
00:01:07.353 --> 00:01:11.753
When one cell repeatedly takes part in firing another, it becomes more effective at

21
00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:14.053
firing it. Now apply the rule here.

22
00:01:14.593 --> 00:01:16.433
The neighbours fire in the same wave.

23
00:01:17.013 --> 00:01:19.913
Together they drive the thalamic cell, so they are strengthened.

24
00:01:20.593 --> 00:01:23.393
The other eye's input rarely helps, so it is weakened.

25
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.607
Repeat this for every thalamic cell.

26
00:01:27.227 --> 00:01:29.447
The two eyes sort into separate territories.

27
00:01:30.027 --> 00:01:32.827
And neighbours within each eye end up next to each other.

28
00:01:33.207 --> 00:01:35.587
So the waves carry two kinds of information.

29
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:39.547
Which eye an input comes from, and roughly where in that eye.

30
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.800
But is it the amount of activity, or its pattern?

31
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.780
In one study, mice lacking the beta2 subunit of the nicotinic receptor had no waves

32
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:48.780
in the first week.

33
00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.700
Their ganglion cells still fired at high rates, but without correlation.

34
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.220
The map in the midbrain stayed broad.

35
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.940
Later work found altered waves in these mice under some conditions.

36
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.980
So they lack normal waves.

37
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.900
Keep the rule. Inputs that fire in step are kept, and the rest are weakened.
